<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takht-e Soleyman</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takht-e Soleyman is an archaeological site in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> province, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, built around a spring-fed lake on a hilltop. It held the main Sasanian sanctuary of the sacred fire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp">Adur Gushnasp</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> sacked in 624, and in the thirteenth century the Ilkhanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> built a summer palace there. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takht-e-soleyman.jpg" alt="Takht-e Soleyman" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elaheabed · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takhte_Soleyman_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
